let's make way for Sam Miller enjoyed  the column the article is welcome to the  era of terrible horrible no good very  bad 30 homerun hitters all right you had  me at the the title there Sam welcome to  the program thanks for joining us all  right is this a good thing or bad thing  with what's happened with all these home  runs this year  you know it's what to say because the  fact is that baseball was already going  to this sort of slow and stagnant style  of play just because pitchers throw so  hard because there are so many good  pitchers that bull pens are sick with 99  mile an hour fastball throwers and teams  that are trying to win are simply going  to load up on pitchers who can get  strikeouts and pitchers are gonna try to  strike guys out and so baseball was  already getting sort of slow and  inactive and without the homeruns I  think we'd probably be in a place where  offense was so low that team would fall  behind 3 2 1 in the fifth inning and  there really would be no point in still  watching now that said I think that the  incentives have also caused a lot more  players to swing for the fences in a way  that a lot of people not just purist but  casual fans as well find a little bit  aesthetically boring and it is kind of  often sand the defensive sort of spurred  each other into this arms race of boom  or bust and I don't know that it's the  most exciting style of playing but I'm  very confident it's not the most  exciting cylla play that said homeruns  you know they're right now they're  keeping the game afloat a little bit and  in the examples that I cited with some  of these players they're keeping careers  going that might otherwise have been  endangered why the sudden burst rise in  homeruns  now I know we've heard a lot of reasons  if I said what's the number one overall  reason Sam why we've had you know this  explosion in homeruns well the number  one overall reason is most likely that  in the summer of 2015  around the all-star break something  changed in a manufacturer of baseballs  I'm not saying it was conspiracy I'm not  saying it was intentional but I believe  that  most likely the evidence suggests that  most likely teams got new baseballs  those baseball's had probably slightly  lower seams the courage the you know the  resistance of balls went down a little  bit and that small effect I had caused a  lot more home runs and I think players  are able to sense that home runs are a  little cheaper now and you've seen more  players go for home runs and you know  try to get more fly balls and maybe  swing a little bit more aggressively but  I think the primary cause is probably  there's no proof of this but there's  really good evidence strong evidence  that the baseball changed a little bit  but Sam can the manufacturer do  something without permission from Major  League Baseball well it's interesting  because the specifications that baseball  gives to manufacturers are sufficiently  broad that within those specifications  within those specifications you're  talking about baseball's that might  travel 20 or 30 extra feet depending on  which extreme of the specifications you  are in it's been the case Allen Nathan  is a physicist who has done a lot of  work on baseball in general and  baseball's specifically and he's shown  that within the same batch you can even  have two baseball's that might travel 15  feet differently based on small really  very small things I really don't think  that there's a conspiracy here I don't  think that this was intentional by the  manufacturer or baseball and again we're  talking about something that is so far  there's not completely convincing  evidence that this is even happening  baseball hasn't acknowledged that this  has happened but the answer to your  question is yes there without even  trying a very small change could could  cause relatively large differences in  the way the baseballs played  we're talking to Sam Miller national  baseball columnist feature writer 