o  protect the fans or for that matter the  pitchers or perhaps even the catchers  and what troubles me is that eventually  something really severely tragic is  going to happen and then they'll act and  it just feels like this is a situation  netting in front of some seats to me  doesn't seem like something that you  need to really fight that  hard against you know as my friend Harry  pallidus pointed out yesterday the most  expensive seats in the stadium are also  the ones behind netting right now which  suggests strongly that consumers get  used to it that it isn't a significant  construction in the game  I think there's been momentum toward  more netting obviously in the last few  years and I just really like to see  people fix problems before the terrible  terrible terrible thing happens and I  don't know if yesterday was a wake-up  call that was a really hard team to  watch you know and you know I got a lot  more attention than the average foul  ball but you know it's putting some  people in danger on almost every touch  Sam thank you enjoyed the column good  luck the rest of the season thanks for  joining us you're welcome that's Sam  Miller  he's with the mothership now feature  writer for them baseball columnist the  Dan Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience
 

